fix(usb): give the debugger a receive path that does not need the transmit gate

kebab_usb_getc() is the byte source for serial KDP, and the CDC-ACM rewrite had
put it behind the same single-owner CPU gate as the console flusher, the log
pump and the terminal panic loop.  That gate is deliberately non-recursive and
is fenced off by ud.panic_cpu, so a debugger read could be refused silently: no
poll, no error, just -1.  Whoever held the port - the panic pump, a console
flush, the event thread, or the permanent panic-CPU fence - could therefore stop
the kernel from ever seeing a request, and a refused read looks exactly like an
idle host from the outside.

Receiving is a different right from transmitting.  Event dispatch and the
bulk-OUT re-arm now run under their own receive lock: any running CPU may take
it, it never consults ud.panic_cpu, and it is re-entrant for the CPU that holds
it, so a receive entered on top of a transmit-critical section (the panic pump
polling, then a debugger hand-back on the same stack) cannot deadlock against
itself.  Transmission stays single-owner: the transmit gate is unchanged except
that its owner may re-enter it, and the one-way ordering rule - receive may be
taken while holding transmit, never the reverse - is what keeps the two locks
from forming a cycle.  The panic takeover now also fences a receive lock whose
owner is a demonstrably halted CPU, with the same controller reset it already
used for a stranded transmit owner.

The panic pump used to hand the byte stream back to KDP only when the first
queued byte was 0xFA.  One stale console byte or an earlier fragment in front of
a fresh frame therefore wedged the hand-back for the rest of the boot, while the
stale bytes were never consumed either.  It now scans the ring for a frame
start, which is unambiguous because the serializer escapes 0xFA everywhere
inside a frame, and KDP's deserializer discards anything before it.

Both directions are now counted in the KEBAB KDP STATE line next to the EP0
state: host_in and in_drop for bulk-OUT bytes, kdp_in for bytes handed to the
debugger reader, kdp_frames/kdp_out for answered frames, and the lock owners.
The next hardware session can then tell "no bytes arrived" from "bytes arrived
and were not answered" instead of guessing.  The EP0 state line itself is
unchanged, so the event accounting (events * 4 == acked) and the existing probe
decoder keep working.

